I’m happy to show you how you would call the `random_forest_train` function four times with the parameter sets you described. Since I don’t have access to your actual `data_random_forest` dataframe here, I’ll use a placeholder string `"data_random_forest"` to represent the data that you would normally pass in.

```json
{
  "function": "random_forest_train",
  "arguments": {
    "n_estimators": 100,
    "max_depth": 10,
    "data": "data_random_forest"
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "function": "random_forest_train",
  "arguments": {
    "n_estimators": 200,
    "max_depth": 20,
    "data": "data_random_forest"
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "function": "random_forest_train",
  "arguments": {
    "n_estimators": 300,
    "max_depth": 30,
    "data": "data_random_forest"
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "function": "random_forest_train",
  "arguments": {
    "n_estimators": 400,
    "max_depth": 40,
    "data": "data_random_forest"
  }
}
```

Once you run each of these calls in your environment (where `data_random_forest` is an actual pandas DataFrame), you will receive four trained model objects. You can then evaluate each model on a validation set or using cross‑validation to obtain metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, F1‑score, etc., and compare them to see how increasing the number of trees and the maximum depth affects performance.
